WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.420
Hey, welcome to Models and Agents for Beginners, episode 112 for July 24th, 2026.

00:00:07.380 --> 00:00:12.020
Today's AI news is pretty exciting, and I promise, no jargon. Let's go.

00:00:12.940 --> 00:00:17.180
You know that moment when your hands are busy, and you just want to speak your thoughts out loud

00:00:17.180 --> 00:00:22.460
instead of hunting for the right keys. Open AI just made that possible on your computer,

00:00:22.740 --> 00:00:28.140
with a new voice feature inside the chat GPT desktop app for both Mac and Windows.

00:00:28.780 --> 00:00:35.140
The system is called GPT Live, and it lets you talk directly to several AI agents at the same time

00:00:35.140 --> 00:00:40.980
while they handle tasks across different apps. Picture a helpful friend sitting beside your desk

00:00:40.980 --> 00:00:47.020
who watches your screen and jumps in the second you speak. You describe an idea for a history project,

00:00:47.300 --> 00:00:52.760
and the voice system turns that into instructions for one agent pulling sources, another organizing

00:00:52.760 --> 00:00:58.980
notes, and a third suggesting images. No more switching between tabs or typing every single step

00:00:58.980 --> 00:01:04.660
because the agents keep the conversation going for you. This matters for students who find typing

00:01:04.660 --> 00:01:11.280
slow or tiring, and for anyone who thinks best by talking through ideas. It turns AI from something

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:17.300
you tap at into something that feels like a real back and forth chat. If you have a plus, pro,

00:01:17.300 --> 00:01:24.540
business, edu, or enterprise plan, open the chat GPT desktop app today and look for the voice button

00:01:24.540 --> 00:01:30.380
to start trying it. Now for the part of the show where we slow down and see what is actually happening

00:01:30.380 --> 00:01:36.680
under the hood. The deep dive today explains how the voice system turns spoken words into useful

00:01:36.680 --> 00:01:43.160
actions without you typing anything. Start with the sound coming from your mouth. The AI slices

00:01:43.160 --> 00:01:49.480
those sounds into tiny chunks it has heard many times before, just like a musician recognizing notes

00:01:49.480 --> 00:01:56.060
in a song. It then guesses the most likely next word or phrase the same way your phone predicts what

00:01:56.060 --> 00:02:01.600
you will text next. At the same time it checks those guest words against whatever is open on your

00:02:01.600 --> 00:02:08.160
screen so the answer fits the actual task. When it is time to reply the process flips around.

00:02:08.720 --> 00:02:14.640
The AI plans its answer first, then turns that plan into natural sounding speech instead of flat

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:21.480
robot tones. Because the loop runs without stopping, the system can juggle several agents at once and

00:02:21.480 --> 00:02:27.700
remember what you said earlier without repetition. The result feels less like issuing commands and more

00:02:27.700 --> 00:02:33.640
like having an ongoing conversation with your computer. That continuous back and forth is what makes

00:02:33.640 --> 00:02:39.640
the new desktop voice feature work so smoothly. Okay, now let us look at three other tools worth

00:02:39.640 --> 00:02:47.080
trying today. First up is a new video tool from Black Forest Labs called Flux3. It creates short

00:02:47.080 --> 00:02:52.580
videos up to 20 seconds long that already include matching audio from the very first frame.

00:02:53.280 --> 00:02:59.680
Earlier tools made silent clips and added sound afterward, which often felt mismatched. Imagine

00:02:59.680 --> 00:03:05.740
building a quick TikTok style story where the character speaks and sound effects appear at the exact

00:03:05.740 --> 00:03:12.120
right moment without extra editing steps. You can watch the latest examples by searching for Flux3

00:03:12.120 --> 00:03:18.980
demos on YouTube or visiting the decoder site to see the quality for yourself. Next is a free learning

00:03:18.980 --> 00:03:25.120
project started by a Reddit user who trained a small model on a regular home computer. The site

00:03:25.120 --> 00:03:30.760
walks through every stage of building a language model with simple code examples, math breakdowns

00:03:30.760 --> 00:03:37.020
and clear diagrams. You do not need any coding background at the start. Just read the explanations

00:03:37.020 --> 00:03:42.300
and follow the visuals to see how each piece connects inside something like chat GPT.

00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:50.160
If you want early access, send a private message to the original poster on the r-slash chat GPT subreddit.

00:03:50.720 --> 00:03:56.640
Third is Gemini Spark from Google, which is now open to more users through ordinary Google accounts.

00:03:57.100 --> 00:04:02.540
It acts as a helper that can break big requests into smaller steps, such as planning a weekend

00:04:02.540 --> 00:04:10.060
study schedule with brakes built in. Go to Gemini.google.com, start a new chat and type a request like

00:04:10.060 --> 00:04:16.220
planning a study schedule that includes fun activities. Watch how it splits the task into clear parts

00:04:16.220 --> 00:04:22.440
you can follow right away. Two quick updates before we wrap up. Amazon updated Alexa Plus,

00:04:22.700 --> 00:04:28.820
so it works with more smart home brands like Bosch, iRobot and Yale. You can now ask in plain

00:04:28.820 --> 00:04:34.120
speech for lights to turn on or a vacuum to start instead of using exact command phrases.

00:04:34.900 --> 00:04:41.160
Open AI also added voice support to the Codex iOS app, letting you control your computer remotely

00:04:41.160 --> 00:04:47.800
from your phone. Android support is on the way soon, and that is a wrap. If any of today's stories

